From: Panurus maintainers
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] coder, lin-proofs: bounds-checked decoding and encoding

The decoders of src/coder.c scan unary codes (coder_dec_ghint,
coder_dec_grandom) and read fixed-width fields without knowing where the
input ends, so a crafted proof makes the verifier read past the end of its
buffer (any remote signer can trigger this). The encoders likewise write
without knowing the capacity of the output.

Add an optional bound to coder_state_t:
- coder_dec_begin_bnd / coder_enc_begin_bnd start a coder that never reads
  or writes at or past in + inlen / out + outlen;
- an access that would do so sets a sticky error flag (coder_get_err), every
  later operation is a no-op and coder_dec_end fails;
- every read and write path is checked: bytes, urandom (fixed width), ghint,
  grandom (unary + binary), end marker, and the sparse urandom5/6 (whose
  header-controlled length is also checked against nelems_max).
coder_dec_begin / coder_enc_begin keep the previous, unbounded behaviour.

lin-proofs: lnp_tbox_decproof / lnp_tbox_encproof take a length,
lin_verifier_verify_ctx_bnd reads at most prooflen bytes and
lin_prover_prove_ctx_bnd fails (returns 0) instead of overflowing when the
proof does not fit. The unbounded entry points are wrappers passing
SIZE_MAX.

Panurus-local patch, applied by `make lazer-install` (lazer.mk).

